# Open Run configuration.
#
# Copy to `.env` and edit. Every value here has a working default — Open Run
# runs with no .env file at all. Nothing in this file is a secret; agent CLI
# credentials come from the CLIs' own logins, and webhook secrets are stored
# in the local database, not here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where Open Run keeps its state
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# App-managed clones, worktrees and the access token live here.
# Default: ~/.openrun (falls back to ~/.agentops if that directory already exists)
# OPENRUN_HOME=/Users/you/.openrun

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network binding  — read SECURITY.md before changing these
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Open Run runs agent CLIs as child processes with your credentials. Anyone who
# can reach this server can run commands as you. It binds to loopback by
# default for exactly that reason.

# Interface to bind. Default: 127.0.0.1
# Setting a non-loopback address (0.0.0.0, a LAN IP) REQUIRES OPENRUN_ACCESS_TOKEN
# to be set as well — the server refuses to start otherwise.
# OPENRUN_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Port. Default: 3000
# PORT=3000

# Shared secret required on every request when bound to a non-loopback address.
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
# Leave unset for normal loopback-only use.
# OPENRUN_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# Extra hostnames Open Run will answer to, comma-separated.
#
# On a loopback bind, Open Run rejects any request whose Host header is not a
# loopback name. That is the DNS-rebinding guard: a web page you visit can make
# its own domain resolve to 127.0.0.1 and then talk to Open Run as if it were
# same-origin, and the Host header is what survives that trick. Signed webhook
# endpoints are exempt, since a third party addresses those by whatever name
# your tunnel has.
#
# Set this only if you reach Open Run through a tunnel or reverse proxy by some
# other name — for example a Cloudflare or ngrok hostname for mobile pairing.
# OPENRUN_ALLOWED_HOSTS=my-tunnel.trycloudflare.com,openrun.internal

# Escape hatch: bind to a non-loopback address with NO access token. This makes
# the machine remotely controllable by anyone who can reach the port. Only ever
# set this when the port is already protected by something else (a private
# network namespace, an authenticating reverse proxy, a VPN with no other
# tenants). It prints a warning on every boot.
# OPENRUN_ALLOW_INSECURE_HOST=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Links
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Base URL used when Open Run builds links back to itself (notifications,
# webhook install instructions). Default: http://localhost:<port>
# OPENRUN_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional commercial control plane
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Unset uses the baked-in default (https://openrun.sh). Sign in is
# optional — every local feature works without an account. Set to `off` to hide
# Sign in entirely. Point at wrangler for local control-plane development:
# AGENTOPS_CLOUD_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787
# AGENTOPS_CLOUD_URL=off
# AGENTOPS_CLOUD_TOKEN=
